1
00:00:05,469 --> 00:00:07,790
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,791 --> 00:00:17,911
This time, based on what we learned in Chapter 3, part 1, let's set up lighting and learn about the methods and principles of applying it to a drawing.

3
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:23,870
This time, we'll go through it with a hands-on exercise.

4
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:27,390
First, let's set up properties like the position and color of the light source.

5
00:00:27,391 --> 00:00:30,270
We'll set up a light source using the qualities of nature.

6
00:00:30,271 --> 00:00:34,350
I'll set two light sources at this diagonal.

7
00:00:34,351 --> 00:00:36,670
It's a warm light close to white.

8
00:00:36,671 --> 00:00:40,910
And to bring out the three-dimensionality even more, I'll use a blue reflected light on the opposite side.

9
00:00:41,765 --> 00:00:44,190
So the color and light aren't too strong.

10
00:00:44,191 --> 00:00:50,391
In reality, sky light doesn't come up from below like this, but for a pictorial expression, I'll set it up this way.

11
00:00:50,895 --> 00:00:54,410
Applying this to a drawing three-dimensionally is hard.

12
00:00:54,411 --> 00:00:57,050
When I was a beginner, I was lost too.

13
00:00:57,051 --> 00:00:59,130
First, let's draw a cube.

14
00:00:59,855 --> 00:01:04,410
Based on the light I set, let's color the cube.

15
00:01:04,411 --> 00:01:08,970
It's good to use a material that has a moderate amount of diffuse reflection.

16
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:11,050
Think of plaster of paris.

17
00:01:11,051 --> 00:01:15,610
When I ask students to draw a cube, they all draw it well.

18
00:01:15,611 --> 00:01:17,930
But what if there are several cubes?

19
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:21,370
If there are many, can you draw them accurately?

20
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:24,850
I drew several cubes like this.

21
00:01:25,134 --> 00:01:27,530
Now I need to color them.

22
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:31,610
Shall we learn the principle of coloring accurately according to light?

23
00:01:31,611 --> 00:01:35,690
I drew several cubes from the side, in two dimensions.

24
00:01:36,309 --> 00:01:40,090
Since it's two-dimensional, they appear as rectangles.

25
00:01:40,091 --> 00:01:44,490
Then let's set the position of the observer and the position of the light source.

26
00:01:45,979 --> 00:01:49,950
Usually I set it to sunlight and draw, in many cases.

27
00:01:49,951 --> 00:01:54,750
If it's sunlight, you can't mark the light source's position in two dimensions.

28
00:01:54,830 --> 00:01:57,790
Because it's a hundred and fifty million kilometers away.

29
00:01:57,791 --> 00:02:01,070
But you can draw the direction the light comes from.

30
00:02:01,445 --> 00:02:06,110
Because the sun is far, the light comes at a uniform angle.

31
00:02:06,111 --> 00:02:11,390
Let's set it so it comes uniformly at a forty-five degree angle like this.

32
00:02:11,391 --> 00:02:17,290
Then let's think about where the light ray hits the box and reflects back.

33
00:02:17,291 --> 00:02:32,010
As we learned in Chapter 3, part 1, the line drawn perpendicular to the surface is called the normal. An incoming light ray, entering at an angle of incidence based on this normal, comes out as a reflected ray at an angle of reflection equal to the angle of incidence.

34
00:02:32,011 --> 00:02:33,610
Like this.

35
00:02:33,611 --> 00:02:41,091
However, since it's a material that undergoes diffuse reflection, I've arbitrarily represented the reflected ray as a circular shape.

36
00:02:42,654 --> 00:02:44,570
Let me call this a ray circle.

37
00:02:45,265 --> 00:02:52,170
The closer to the reflected ray, the stronger the light, and the farther, the weaker the light.

38
00:02:52,171 --> 00:02:59,491
Using the position where this ray circle reaches the observer, you can determine the brightness of the object.

39
00:02:59,614 --> 00:03:04,010
The closer it is to this warm color, the stronger the light the observer feels.

40
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:09,850
You might want to consider total internal reflection too, but let's keep it simple like this.

41
00:03:09,851 --> 00:03:14,951
At this time, if the reflected ray reaches the observer directly, it becomes a highlight.

42
00:03:15,475 --> 00:03:16,475
At the very top.

43
00:03:17,614 --> 00:03:21,080
The front of the cube becomes the highlight.

44
00:03:21,081 --> 00:03:25,361
The rest doesn't get a highlight.

45
00:03:26,970 --> 00:03:35,820
If you move this ray circle around and compare, you can see the light getting weaker as you go down.

46
00:03:35,821 --> 00:03:37,980
Let's compare the top face too.

47
00:03:37,981 --> 00:03:43,500
For these three cubes on top, the observer wouldn't see the top face, right?

48
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:45,260
Let's look at the fourth cube.

49
00:03:50,734 --> 00:03:53,800
The light hits around here in the ray circle.

50
00:03:55,070 --> 00:04:04,480
You can see the bottom cube is brighter than the one above it, because the observer's position is closer to the reflected ray.

51
00:04:04,481 --> 00:04:15,320
If we turn this into three dimensions, it gets a bit more complex, but the principle is the same, so let's recall the above and try to draw as accurately as possible.

52
00:04:15,321 --> 00:04:19,041
Once you've practiced, change the cube to a sphere and draw it.

53
00:04:19,725 --> 00:04:24,120
Unlike the cube, you can mark a highlight on every part of a sphere.

54
00:04:25,630 --> 00:04:37,400
If you draw this accurately, you'll see the highlight position gets higher the lower and closer the sphere is.

55
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:48,311
What if, instead of sunlight coming from very far away, we switch to a nearby artificial light?

56
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:52,830
The light can come like this.

57
00:04:52,831 --> 00:04:56,551
The position of the highlight has become more fickle.

58
00:04:59,230 --> 00:05:07,641
Light varies with intensity and angle, but you should remember it also varies with the position of the light source.

59
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:16,360
Let's practice applying various lights to this sphere.

60
00:05:16,361 --> 00:05:19,320
Let's also look at materials.

61
00:05:19,321 --> 00:05:24,280
I've been asked a lot how to draw metal.

62
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:27,601
Among them, especially metal with a smooth material.

63
00:05:27,805 --> 00:05:32,520
It doesn't leave the light theory we've learned so far.

64
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:38,920
Smooth metal has a high reflectance and a relatively even surface, so it reflects surrounding objects.

65
00:05:39,575 --> 00:05:40,640
It's said to be reflected.

66
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:46,720
Since we're people who know a certain amount about light, we'll say it reflects.

67
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:53,200
Starting from the surrounding environment, the light reflected off the metal enters our eyes.

68
00:05:53,201 --> 00:05:57,920
Because of that, when drawing metal, you have to consider the surroundings too.

69
00:05:57,921 --> 00:05:59,440
First, let's draw a cylinder.

70
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:03,360
Then let's set it up as if there's something around it.

71
00:06:03,361 --> 00:06:06,840
Let's assume the background is a blue world.

72
00:06:07,315 --> 00:06:10,140
First, there's the observer right in front.

73
00:06:10,141 --> 00:06:12,780
Let's place objects around it, without much meaning.

74
00:06:12,785 --> 00:06:14,460
Like this.

75
00:06:14,461 --> 00:06:16,060
If you look at this from above.

76
00:06:17,285 --> 00:06:19,600
It would look like this.

77
00:06:19,601 --> 00:06:25,520
I drew lines considering the angle of incidence and reflection for the light coming off the object.

78
00:06:25,521 --> 00:06:29,161
The fruit reflects off the cylinder and enters the observer's eyes.

79
00:06:29,870 --> 00:06:32,960
The observer, of course, gets reflected too.

80
00:06:32,961 --> 00:06:34,800
The lighting gets reflected as well.

81
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,440
The mailbox gets reflected too.

82
00:06:39,441 --> 00:06:43,760
The reflected area comes out like this.

83
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:47,040
It's become narrower than the object itself, surprisingly.

84
00:06:48,005 --> 00:06:54,160
That's why objects appear compressed left and right on a cylindrical metal surface.

85
00:06:54,161 --> 00:06:55,161
Like this.

86
00:06:57,270 --> 00:07:02,440
Let's return to three dimensions and draw the objects reflected in the cylinder.

87
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,320
Stretching out the reflected surfaces.

88
00:07:06,705 --> 00:07:10,010
At this time, I'll draw without considering the Y-axis.

89
00:07:10,011 --> 00:07:15,930
Since we're not drawing a mirror, we have to mix in the metal's own color.

90
00:07:15,931 --> 00:07:17,930
I've arbitrarily mixed in a brass color.

91
00:07:19,990 --> 00:07:25,840
For the part where the light comes in, keep the bright light, and for realism, blend it naturally.

92
00:07:28,570 --> 00:07:32,740
This is the basic principle of drawing metal.

93
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:37,150
Other materials aren't much different.

94
00:07:37,151 --> 00:07:46,430
Depending on the polish of the surface, it can reflect honestly like a mirror or smooth metal, or it can reflect only a small part, like paper or skin.

95
00:07:46,431 --> 00:07:55,390
For example, on a smooth surface, the area where light reflects is limited, but on a rough surface like this, it can appear wide.

96
00:07:55,470 --> 00:08:02,510
Spreading wide means the light energy can't concentrate in one place and scatters, so only some reaches our eyes.

97
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:06,450
Which means the contrast of highlights, etc., also decreases.

98
00:08:06,451 --> 00:08:09,090
Let's practice the same way with various materials.

99
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:16,110
Once you master this, you can confidently apply light to the characters we draw too.

100
00:08:19,770 --> 00:08:27,020
This is a drawing of a person, drawn by someone who doesn't understand light well, assuming the light comes down vertically.

101
00:08:27,021 --> 00:08:28,220
What is wrong?

102
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:34,299
People who took this chapter properly will have noticed.

103
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:45,660
The problem with this picture is that, without considering light theory, it just brightened every part facing upward, on the assumption light comes down vertically from above.

104
00:08:47,055 --> 00:08:50,360
Let's look at the side view in two dimensions.

105
00:08:50,361 --> 00:08:53,921
I've marked the points where the highlight should align.

106
00:08:54,710 --> 00:08:56,520
Shall we find the highlight?

107
00:08:56,521 --> 00:08:59,560
It's located a bit more toward the front than you'd think.

108
00:08:59,561 --> 00:09:04,540
And this chest area is blocked from light by the head.

109
00:09:06,595 --> 00:09:10,910
If you color it properly again, it looks like this.

110
00:09:10,911 --> 00:09:19,070
Coloring done with habitual and one-sided thinking builds up from the small things and makes the picture look amateurish.

111
00:09:19,071 --> 00:09:24,990
People who don't understand light often can't render recessed areas properly.

112
00:09:24,991 --> 00:09:34,270
For example, when drawing a face, people often don't know why a highlight is placed in the recessed area between the nose and the eyes.

113
00:09:34,271 --> 00:09:41,630
Highlights also form like this within recessed clothing folds.

114
00:09:41,631 --> 00:09:45,630
If you understand light, you can know the reason.

115
00:09:45,631 --> 00:09:50,350
I've shown the recessed shape in two dimensions.

116
00:09:50,351 --> 00:09:57,790
Check the position of the light, and draw the reflected ray according to the angle of incidence.

117
00:09:57,791 --> 00:10:01,470
Find where the highlight forms.

118
00:10:01,471 --> 00:10:04,951
A highlight definitely forms in the recessed area too.

119
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:10,430
If you draw this in three dimensions, it would look like this.

120
00:10:10,431 --> 00:10:18,270
Not just raised areas, but recessed areas like this, if you understand light, you can consider them properly and draw them.

121
00:10:18,271 --> 00:10:23,310
Are these light theories important in concept art and illustration, you ask?

122
00:10:23,390 --> 00:10:27,950
Realistic light management might not be of great importance to some.

123
00:10:27,951 --> 00:10:31,671
There's a difference between knowing you're omitting it and omitting it without knowing.

124
00:10:31,950 --> 00:10:33,630
However, I've always tried to express things realistically.

125
00:10:33,690 --> 00:10:38,730
And that's what I've been teaching you.

126
00:10:38,810 --> 00:10:47,370
Just as you have to understand facial anatomy to draw character design through omission and exaggeration, I think light is the same.

127
00:10:47,371 --> 00:10:55,770
I hope you'll build on realistic light as a foundation and develop it into various styles through omission and exaggeration.

128
00:10:55,771 --> 00:11:02,890
Based on what we learned this time, next time let's place shapes with light settings on a character sketch.

129
00:11:03,305 --> 00:11:06,811
And we'll have a time to apply and color it.
